src/superclaude/agents/issue-worker-t1.md:15:The files you write under `.dev/issues/` (`scout.md`, `accuracy-check.md`, `issue-summary.md`, the reports under `analysis/` such as `analysis/analyze-<scope>.md`, `flags.md`, `flags-t1.md`, `issue-body.md`, `draft-notes.md`) are input files for the orchestrator's next tool calls (`gh --body-file` posts and `/sc:recommend`), not report files. Claude Code refuses a subagent's Write of any `.md` file whose name starts with REPORT, SUMMARY, FINDINGS or ANALYSIS (in any letter case), which is why none of these names starts with those words. Writing every file your job names with the Write tool is mandatory; returning its content as text instead is a failure (`E-WORKER-NO-OUTPUT`).
src/superclaude/agents/issue-worker-t1.md:21:1. **Accuracy check and summary (claim Wave 4, when `scout.md` sizes the issue `complex`).** Assume the issue text is out of date. Check each concrete claim in the issue body and comments (paths, symbols, behaviours, flags) against today's code, and check each merged pull request and changed file listed in `<D>/scout.md` for relevance. Write `<D>/accuracy-check.md` from the `accuracy-check-comment.md` template with the verdict `current` or `out_of_date` and the evidence for each changed claim. When the verdict is `out_of_date`, run `/sc:analyze --depth deep` on the paths the issue touches and save its report as `<D>/analysis/analyze-<scope>.md` (`<scope>` = the main path's last segment; never a name starting with analysis, report, summary or findings). Always write `<D>/issue-summary.md` from the `issue-summary.md` template: what the issue asks for, the relevant files and systems, type, complexity, the effort estimate in hours using the rubric in `~/.claude/skills/sc-issue-protocol/refs/claim.md` (the in-checkout copy is the project `.claude/skills/sc-issue-protocol/refs/claim.md`; read the installed copy first), and links to anything under `<D>/analysis/`.
